            <p>During the stay in Medina and Mecca, I made every effort not only to capture the blessed places attributed to the pure Imams (A.S) and great personalities with my camera as much as possible, but also to conduct all possible written research about these places. It is not possible to describe all these details here, but you can read in detail about the holy places of Medina and Mecca and the accounts of Hajj in my book "Madinat-ul-Ahl al-Bayt (A.S)". Observing the ancient buildings of Medina, one particular thing that drew my attention was the black, porous stones found here. Upon research, I learned that the holes in these stones are not the result of wear and tear or the cruelty of time, but are a part of the volcanic history of this land. This 'basalt' stone, emerging from the 'Harrah' (volcanic fields) around Medina, surfaced and cooled centuries ago, and the release of trapped gases created these small holes in the chest of these stones, giving them a unique 'breathing' structure. While these stones are more durable than granite and stand firm against the ravages of time, these natural holes inside them acted as an excellent 'thermal insulation' in the intense heat of Medina, keeping the interior temperature of the buildings balanced. This was a natural air-conditioning system that provided comfort to the ancient inhabitants of Medina in the desert heat. When we look at the relics from the era of the Imams of Ahl al-Bayt (A.S), such as the Mosque of Noor, the Palace of Urwah bin Zubayr, or the walls of ancient mosques (which we will mention later), these porous stones tell us how deeply these buildings were connected to their roots. Even today, these stones hold within them the fragrance of the prostrations and footsteps that sanctified this land fourteen hundred years ago. Their durability is proof that whatever the Ahl al-Bayt (A.S) and their companions built, was based on sustainability and immense benefit.</p>
